ails.\n\nTry a different pairing: line through (2,3) and (3,2) slope (2-3)/(3-2) = -1, non-sunny. Not allowed. (2,3)-(4,1) slope -1, not allowed. (2,3)-(2,1) vertical, not allowed. So (2,3) cannot be paired with any other point in a sunny line. So (2,3) must be covered by a sunny line alone. That uses one sunny line. Then we have left (2,1),(3,2),(4,1) to be covered by one sunny line. Can we cover those three with one sunny line? No, a line can cover at most two points (unless they are collinear). Let's see if any line through any two of them is sunny: (2,1)-(3,2) slope 1 (sunny). That line also passes through (4,3) not in set. So it covers (2,1) and (3,2). It does not cover (4,1). So we would need to cover (4,1) also, but we have no lines left. So fails.\n\nThus that configuration fails.\n\nMaybe we can use a different set of non-sunny lines. Let's try to use vertical line x=1, vertical line x=2 (both non-sunny). That's two non-sunny lines covering columns 1 and 2 fully. Then we have two lines left, both must be sunny. The remaining points are columns 3 and 4: (3,1),(3,2),(4,1). That's three points left, to be covered by two sunny lines. As we saw earlier, any line through any two of these is non-sunny (vertical/horizontal/slope -1). So we cannot cover any two with a sunny line. So we need three sunny lines, but we have only two. So fails.\n\nTry vertical line x=1, slope -1 line x+y=4 (covers (1,3),(2,2),(3,1)). Then we have two lines left, both sunny. Let's see uncovered points: x=1 column: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). x+y=4 covers (1,3),(2,2),(3,1). So covered: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(2,2),(3,1). Uncovered: (2,1),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1). Same as before. So we need to cover those four points with two sunny lines. As we saw, seems impossible because (2,3) cannot be paired with any other point in a sunny line. So fails.\n\nTry vertical line x=1, horizontal line y=1 (both non-sunny). Then we have two sunny lines left. Covered points: x=1 covers (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4). y=1 co