ed triples (p1,p2,p3) such that they are collinear and slope not 0,∞,-1. Let's compute slopes between pairs and see if any third point lies on same line.\n\nWe can try to find lines with slope 2: line through (1,1) slope 2: y = 2x -1. Points: (1,1) gives y=1; (2,3) gives y=3; (3,5) not present. So (2,3) is present. So line y = 2x -1 passes through (1,1) and (2,3). That's two points. Does it also pass through (3,5) not present. So only two.\n\nSlope 1/2: line through (1,1) slope 0.5: y = 0.5x + 0.5. Points: (1,1) gives y=1; (3,2) gives y=2; (5,3) not present. So (3,2) present. So line passes through (1,1) and (3,2). That's two points.\n\nSlope -2: line through (1,4) slope -2: y = -2x + 6. Points: (1,4) gives y=4; (2,2) gives y=2; (3,0) not present. So (2,2) present. So line passes through (1,4) and (2,2). That's two points.\n\nSlope -1/2: line through (1,2) slope -0.5: y = -0.5x + 2.5. Points: (1,2) gives y=2; (3,1) gives y=1; (5,0) not present. So (3,1) present. So line passes through (1,2) and (3,1). That's two points.\n\nSlope 3: line through (1,1) slope 3: y = 3x -2. Points: (1,1) gives y=1; (2,4) not present; (3,7) not present. So only one.\n\nSlope -3: line through (1,4) slope -3: y = -3x + 7. Points: (1,4) gives y=4; (2,1) gives y=1; (3,-2) not present. So (2,1) present. So line passes through (1,4) and (2,1). That's two points.\n\nSlope 2/3: line through (1,1) slope 2/3: y = (2/3)x + 1/3. Points: (1,1) gives y=1; (2,5/3) not integer; (3,7/3) not integer. So only one.\n\nSlope -2/3: line through (1,4) slope -2/3: y = -2/3 x + 10/3. Points: (1,4) gives y=4; (2,8/3) not integer; (3,2) gives y=2? Let's compute: y = -2/3*3 + 10/3 = -2 + 10/3 = ( -6 + 10)/3 = 4/3, not 2. So not.\n\nSlope 1/3: line through (1,1) slope 1/3: y = (1/3)x + 2/3. Points: (1,1) gives y=1; (2,4/3) not integer; (3,5/3) not integer; (4,2) not present? Actually (4,2) not present because a+b=6 >5. So only one.\n\nSlope -1/3: line through (1,4) slope -1/3: y = -1/3 x + 13/3. Points: (1,4) gives 